know there are four of each rank. Let\'s see how many Ks are out there. I have one, and my turn says someone played a K. That means, there are two unknown. But the prompt does not list it as such. Strange. Let\'s dig deeper. The same can be said for 2s. If there are unknowns, the AI prompt should list them. So, the prompt is saying Ks and 2s are exhausted. It is missing from the list. If it\'s missing, can I assume it is zero? Maybe. I think that the unlisted ones are not relevant or high cards.\n\nOkay, if I\'m interpreting that right and I can assume that there are no remaining Ks or 2s, then an Ace is the absolute highest single.\n\nHere\'s my hand: 7, 9, K, A. I\'ve got to decide the order of play. I can\'t just play my A, because then I can\'t lead with my small cards. I need to sneak them in. But, if I play a small card like a 7, my teammate can play a bigger card, and take control, while the landlord may not be able to. The goal is to either go out, or have my teammate go out. I should start with the lowest card. So, should I play the 7?\n\nWell, if I play the 7, I have the potential to get rid of some garbage cards. Also, the strategy is not to play A first. My teammate has 15 cards, so a 7 would be easily beatable, which is my goal.\n\nNow, let\'s play the 7 and see what happens.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: I currently hold four single cards: 7, 9, K, and A. I have the "Free Play" initiative. My teammate (Next Player) has 15 cards, and the Landlord (Previous Player) has 12 cards. Since my teammate plays immediately after me, my goal is to assist them in emptying their hand or maintaining control.\n\nReason: Playing the lowest single (♣7) is the optimal cooperative move. It allows my teammate to easily beat my card with a middle-rank card (like a 10, J, or Q) and take the lead to play out their own hand. This forces the Landlord to either pass (giving teammate control) or spend a high card to intervene. I should save my Ace and King to potentially beat the Landlord\'s high cards later and protect my teammate. Playing the Ace now would isolate me with weak cards (7, 9, K) and no re-entry control.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♣7"]}'}
